  "answers": {
    "traditional_rag": "**Dysbiosis → periodontitis (local bone loss)**  \n- Human metatranscriptome profiling of sub‑gingival plaque identified a distinct dysbiotic signature (up‑regulated motility, lipid A, peptidoglycan, iron‑transport genes) that preceded clinical progression and alveolar bone loss【PMID:25918553】 (human).  \n- Review of oral pathobionts describes how cooperative interactions among a dysbiotic community generate potent immunostimulatory activity that directly drives alveolar bone loss【PMID:24646638】 (human observation).  \n- Animal studies show that community‑level dysbiosis, rather than single taxa, initiates periodontal bone loss and that bacterial challenge is necessary for the inflammatory cascade【PMID:25574929】 (mouse/rat).\n\n**Periodontitis → inflammatory mediators**  \n- In murine models, bacterial challenge provokes key cytokines (IL‑1, TNF‑α, prostaglandins, complement, RANKL) that are required for inflammatory bone destruction【PMID:25574929】 (animal).  \n- A P. gingivalis mouse model demonstrated that reduced hepatic‑X‑receptor expression modulates the macrophage cytokine response"
  },
